1 STOP

   Ends every command procedure, or deletes a process.

   Format

     STOP [process-name]

2 Parameter

 process-name

   The process to delete, as SHOW SYSTEM lists it (quote a name with
   blanks or lowercase).  Without one, and without /IDENTIFICATION,
   STOP ends command procedures instead.

2 Description

   In a command procedure, or after Ctrl/Y has interrupted one, STOP
   with no process ends every command procedure, returning to the
   terminal (command level 0); $STATUS is left as it was.  ON ERROR THEN
   STOP in a nested procedure therefore doesn't return to the procedure
   that ran it.  At the terminal, STOP with no process ends an image
   Ctrl/C or Ctrl/Y left stopped.

   With a process, STOP deletes it, and the subprocesses it created
   before it.  Its files are closed, its termination mailbox (if it had
   one) is sent the termination message, with the final status
   %SYSTEM-F-ABORT, and a process suspended is resumed first so that it
   can end.  Process 1, the console's own process, can't be stopped;
   EXIT ends the session.  INITIALIZE, VMINIT, ZERO, and leaving govax
   delete every process but process 1 in the same way.

2 Qualifiers
/EXIT

      /[NO]EXIT[=mode]

   The exit handlers to run before the process is deleted: those of the
   mode and the more privileged modes (KERNEL_MODE, EXECUTIVE_MODE, the
   default, SUPERVISOR_MODE, USER_MODE).  /EXIT=USER_MODE makes the
   image exit, as /IMAGE does, and deletes the process when it has.
   govax's images have exit handlers only in user mode, so the other
   modes and /NOEXIT delete the process at once.

/IDENTIFICATION

      /IDENTIFICATION=pid

   Names the process by its process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
   lists it.

/IMAGE

   Ends the process's image, not the process: the image exits as $FORCEX
   makes it, its exit handlers called, with the status
   %SYSTEM-F-FORCEDEXIT, and a subprocess's DCL goes on with its next
   command.  Without a process, STOP/IMAGE is STOP.

2 Examples

   1.$ STOP/IDENTIFICATION=00000305

     Deletes the process 00000305.

   2.$ STOP/IMAGE LIBRA

     Ends the image LIBRA runs.

   3.$ STOP/EXIT=USER_MODE LIBRA

     Runs the user-mode exit handlers of LIBRA's image, then deletes
     LIBRA.
